<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Rumfa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1463–<a href="/years/1499">1499</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Rumfa was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> from about 1463 to 1499. He extended the city walls, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a> palace, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmi">Kurmi</a> market and strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> at court, receiving the North African scholar al-Maghili, who wrote him a treatise on government. His reign made Kano a leading centre of trade and learning.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rumfa's birth name was Muhammad dan Yakubu. His lineage is much debated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> calls him the son of Yakubu, the nineteenth ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, and of Fasima Berana, a Kanuri name, while other sources make Rumfa, Yakubu and Abdullahi Burja half-brothers and give his mother as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, a woman from Rano. Some historians think the line came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a> by way of Gaya, and that it took power with the help of Gaya and possibly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>. Oral tradition explains the name Rumfa by the sheds (rumfa) built in his time to keep food out of reach of wild dogs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rumfa succeeded his father in 1463 and inherited a growing economy that drew scholars and traders of Arab, Kanuri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> descent. The best known was Muhammad Al-Maghili, who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> after a stay in Katsina. Under his guidance Rumfa built mosques, cut down a sacred tree of the Abagayawa and raised a minaret on its site, banned pagan practices, and in his reign Eid al-Fitr was first held in Kano. Al-Maghili wrote On the Obligation of Princes as a guide for him.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> credits him with twelve innovations. Among them were the Taran Kano, a council of nine advisers and kingmakers, the practice of kulle or purdah, and the giving of offices of state and the treasury to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and slaves. He began the custom of taking every first-born virgin among the Indabawa for himself and is said to have had a thousand wives. His court used the kakaki trumpet and ostrich-feather sandals. He fought the first war between Kano and Katsina, which lasted eleven years without a victor.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>'s account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> is thought to describe Rumfa's time, with its wealthy merchants, skilled craftsmen and cavalry. Leo also reported a conquest of Kano by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> under Askiya the Great, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> does not mention it, and historians such as Humphrey J Fisher doubt that it happened or think it had no lasting effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> calls him just and learned and says that there was no Sarki more powerful. The Rumfawa, the rulers of his line, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> until the Kutumbawa replaced them in 1623, and the first Kanoan Empire arose under his grandson Muhammad Kisoki. Kano's palace still bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Rumfa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Every capital was protected by massive earthen walls with fortified gates, enclosing farmland and wells so that the population could withstand sieges; the walls of Kano, extended under <b>Muhammad Rumfa</b> in the fifteenth century, ran for many kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…roads were opened from Bornu to Gonja, the source of kola nuts in what is now Ghana, and the king became the first in Hausaland to own camels; a prince from Bornu founded the market of Karabka; and <b>Muhammad Rumfa</b> established the Kurmi market, which still trades today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
